   "markdown": "Summation formula:\n\n\\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i = x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n\\) (9)\n\n\\(\\iint_D f(x, y) \\, dx \\, dy\\) (15)\n\n\\(\\text{Integral formula:}\\)\n\n\\(\\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx = F(b) - F(a)\\) (10)\n\n\\(\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{n}\\right)^n = e\\) (16)\n\n\\(\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin x}{x} = 1\\) (11)\n\n\\(\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin x}{x} = 1\\) (11)\n\n\\(\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{a}{b} = \\frac{a}{b} = 1\\) (17)\n\n\\(\\text{Aligned multi-line formula:}\\)\n\n\\((a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\\) (12)\n\n\\((a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2\\) (13)\n\n\\(\\text{Complex summation:}\\)\n\n\\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\sum_{j=1}^{m} a_{ij} x_j = b_i\\) (14)\n\n\\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\) (19)\n\n\\(\\text{In line formula:}\\)\n\n\\(\\lim_{i \\to 1} \\sum_{j=1}^{n} a_{ij} x_j = b_i\\) (14)\n\n\\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\) (20)\n\n1.3\n\nIn mathematics, the quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) can be solved using the formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where the discriminant \\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\) determines the nature of the roots. When \\(\\Delta > 0\\), there are two distinct real roots; when \\(\\Delta = 0\\), there is exactly one real root; and when \\(\\Delta < 0\\), the roots are complex conjugates.\n\nFor sequences, the arithmetic series \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i\\) can be expressed as \\(\\frac{n(a_1 + a_n)}{2}\\), while the geometric series \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} ar^k\\) converges to \\(\\frac{a}{1-r}\\) when \\(|r| < 1\\). In calculus, the derivative \\(f'(x) = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\\) represents the instantaneous rate of change, and the definite integral \\(\\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx\\) gives the accumulated area under the curve.\n\nIn probability theory, if \\(X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)\\), then the expected value \\(\\mathbb{E}[X] = \\mu\\) and the variance \\(\\text{Var}(X) = \\sigma^2\\). The standard normal distribution has the density function \\(\\phi(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-x^2/2}\\), and its cumulative distribution function is denoted by \\(\\Phi(x)\\).\n\nFor vectors in \\(\\mathbb{R}^n\\), the dot product \\(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} u_i v_i\\) measures the angle between vectors, while the norm \\(\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^{n} v_i^2}\\) gives the Euclidean length. A matrix \\(A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}\\) is orthogonal if \\(A^T A = I_n\\), where \\(I_n\\) is the \\(n \\times n\\) identity matrix.\n\nIn information theory, the entropy \\(H(X) = -\\sum_i p(x_i) \\log p(x_i)\\) quantifies\n\n2",
